{"id":"R0062","status":"READ","pdf_page":5,"locator":"Fig. 4 and Experiment Setup (p. 5); Fig. 6 and Effect of UAV Unavailability (p. 6)","setting":"physical_prototype","excerpt":"This prototype system contains four UAVs, an NVIDIA Jetson Nano developer kit is set on UAV#1 as computing unit, and an NVIDIA Jetson TX2 as well as two Jetson Nanos are deployed on other three UAVs respectively.","qualification":"Measurement setting is a physical hardware prototype of four airborne embedded computing and communication units (three NVIDIA Jetson Nanos and one NVIDIA Jetson TX2, Fig. 4, p. 5) running Faster R-CNN on a video stream; failure handling is tested separately via a deliberately imposed software disconnection by disabling and later recovering UAV#3's communication program (p. 6, Fig. 6)."}
{"id":"R0125","status":"READ","pdf_page":3,"locator":"Section III.D Experimental Configuration and Section IV Experimental Results","setting":"host_emulation","excerpt":"Experiments were conducted on a workstation equipped with an AMD Ryzen 9 7900X processor providing 24 logical cores at 4.7 GHz base frequency. This configuration simulates a fleet of UAV workers with varying computational capabilities by allocating different thread counts to each worker process.","qualification":"Despite the authors' word 'simulates,' Section IV evaluates real ONNX Runtime YOLO11m inference and HTTP multipart worker-orchestrator execution running as workstation-hosted worker processes on an AMD Ryzen 9 7900X host with controlled thread counts (via threadpoolctl) and 2GB memory per worker, rather than a simulation of measured UAV hardware execution."}
